Ladybird picks the document and image Accept values by navigator
compatibility mode. Chrome mode sends what Chromium sends: for a
navigation, kFrameAcceptHeaderValue (content/common/
content_constants_internal.h) plus the signed-exchange suffix from
content/browser/web_package/signed_exchange_consts.h; for an image,
ImageAcceptHeader() in third_party/blink/common/loader/network_utils.cc.
Gecko mode sends what Firefox sends: DocumentAcceptHeader() and
ImageAcceptHeader() in netwerk/protocol/http/nsHttpHandler.cpp, the
former with the image types its network.http.accept.include_images
pref adds. WebKit mode sends the Fetch spec’s document value, which is
what acceptHeaderValueFromType() in Source/WebCore/loader/cache/
CachedResourceRequest.cpp returns for a main resource, and keeps the
image value Ladybird already sent, since WebKit’s own image value names
formats such as HEIC and JXL that Ladybird doesn’t decode, and claiming
them would have a CDN serve what we can’t show.
